{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED. Independent exact-head review of d4d4d7414da1b70740ebbb7afade6aa9d799bb44 on request/http-connection-timeouts, under review request 63610b5a and promise c7aa7a07, co-signing both artifacts: d31a9c5d at cmd/gs and e7f0cfd8 at docs. I re-derived every claim at that commit and checked the two things I suspected were wrong; one of them I was wrong about, and I say so below.\n\nWHAT THE HEAD IS. git diff --stat main...HEAD is three files, +145/-2: cmd/gs/main.go, cmd/gs/main_test.go, docs/reference/gs/serve.md. residentHTTPServer gives the resident listener and the profiler one shared set of bounds — 5s request headers, 10s whole request, 40s response, 60s idle, 64 KiB headers — replacing two separate literals that set only ReadHeaderTimeout. residentHTTPHandler wraps the resident only, and clears the connection write deadline for exactly one path, /v0/status.\n\nTHE THREE PROPERTIES ARE PINNED, AND I CHECKED THAT BY BREAKING THEM. Each mutation fails a different assertion, so the tests are three separate claims and not one claim written three ways. Making the exception unconditional, `if true` in place of the path test, fails with \"ordinary response survived the 50ms write deadline with status 204\". Removing the exception entirely fails the other half of the same test: \"slow status was cut by the ordinary write deadline: EOF\". Dropping WriteTimeout from residentHTTPServer fails the constants assertion in the slow-body test. So the head does not merely set fields; the fields are load-bearing and the narrowness of the exception is itself asserted.\n\nTHE EVIDENCE IS REAL RATHER THAN SIMULATED, which the request asked me to check specifically. TestResidentHTTPServerBoundsSlowRequestBodies dials a real loopback socket, writes a request declaring Content-Length 100 and sends one byte, and requires a 408 rather than a hang — so the read bound is observed on the wire, not asserted on a struct. TestResidentHTTPServerLetsColdStatusOutliveWriteTimeout runs a real server with a 50ms write deadline and a handler that sleeps past it, then makes two requests through a real client: /v0/status must succeed and /ordinary must fail. The second half is what makes the test discriminating; a test that only checked /v0/status would pass under the unconditional mutation above.\n\nGATES, run by me at the exact head with a clean worktree. gofmt -l over cmd, internal, host and spike: no output. git diff --check main...HEAD: clean. make vet: clean. make build: both binaries. go test ./... -count=1: 27 packages ok, 0 FAIL, internal/apphost has no test files. go test -race -count=1 ./cmd/gs: ok.\n\nTHE DOCUMENTED NUMBERS ARE THE CODE'S NUMBERS. I checked each one rather than reading the prose as a summary. The five limits in serve.md match the five constants. \"Resident JSON decoding also stops after 2 MiB\" is internal/service/server.go:762, http.MaxBytesReader with 2\u003c\u003c20. \"the bounded long poll, whose accepted timeout is at most thirty seconds\" is Poll at internal/service/server.go:378, which accepts a timeout only up to 30s and otherwise uses 25s — so the long poll's own ceiling sits 10s inside the 40s response deadline, and the two bounds cannot collide. \"Profiler routes have no exception\" is true in the code: serveProfiler calls residentHTTPServer(mux) with no residentHTTPHandler wrapper.\n\nA CONCERN I RAISED AND THEN REFUTED, recorded because the refutation is the useful part. The profiler previously had no WriteTimeout and now inherits 40s, so I expected `go tool pprof -seconds 60` to start truncating, and I was ready to call that an undocumented regression. I wrote a throwaway test instead of reasoning: a 1-second profile against residentHTTPServer with a 300ms WriteTimeout returns 200, not an error. The reason is in the standard library. net/http/pprof/pprof.go:126-130 reads the server's WriteTimeout from the request context and, when it is non-zero, extends the write deadline to WriteTimeout plus the requested duration for that request. So bounding the profiler does not cap profile or trace length; the package compensates. There is no regression here and no documentation gap. I would have filed a false finding on reasoning alone.\n\nARCHITECTURE. The affected layers are Surface and composition (cmd/gs), which owns serveCommand and serveProfiler, and the docs page for that surface. Both contracts are preserved and neither changes. This is transport policy at the process boundary: no Workroom concept moves into a kernel package, no signature changes, no dependency is added, and internal/service, internal/kernel and internal/gitstore are untouched — the handler chain is unchanged apart from one wrapper outside TrustedHostHandler and telemetry. Ordering is right and worth stating: residentHTTPHandler sits outermost, so the deadline is cleared before the trusted-host check runs, but clearing a write deadline confers no authority and the refusal still happens, so nothing is admitted that would otherwise be refused. No contract changed, so no update to docs/reference/architecture.md is required at this head and none was made.\n\nSECURITY. The affected boundary is availability of the resident to a slow or stalled local client, and the change closes the hole in the right direction: before it, one unbounded connection could hold a goroutine indefinitely, which loopback does not prevent. Slow-client behaviour: the read bounds now terminate a stalled request body, demonstrated on a real socket. Cold rebuild: the exception exists because abandoning the client does not stop the shared rebuild, so cutting the response at 40s would waste the work and tell the caller nothing, and the head keeps the connection attached instead. Deadline interaction across requests: clearing the deadline cannot leak into the next request on a keep-alive connection, because net/http re-arms it per request in readRequest at net/http/server.go:993-996; I read that rather than assuming it. Trusted-host ordering: examined above, no authority effect. Bounds: header size, request read and idle are all now finite where they were not.\n\nOne honest limit, which I am recording rather than requesting changes over. The /v0/status exception is unbounded, not merely generous: once cleared, nothing in this head bounds that response, and the serve.md sentence \"The read, header-size, and idle bounds still apply\" is true of the connection but none of those three bounds an in-flight status response — the idle timeout governs the gap between requests, not a request in progress. So a client that opens status requests and never reads them can still hold connections. Under the trusted-process posture that is not a security defect: any process inside the boundary can already act as every actor key, so it has far cheaper attacks available. It is a residual availability exposure to a buggy client, and the alternative — a large finite deadline — reintroduces exactly the arbitrary number this change is removing. I would rather the sentence said which bounds do not apply to an in-flight status response than change the code, and that is a sentence, not a new head. I found no unresolved security defect.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. This head is a net simplification, which is the unusual and good case: two divergent literal server constructions collapse into one named function with five named constants, so the resident and the profiler can no longer drift apart, and the one intentional difference between them is a single wrapper that says which path is exceptional and why. There is no knob, no configuration surface and no way to widen the exception by accident — it is an exact string match on one path. Nothing to cut. The only thing I considered asking for is the documentation precision above, and I am not spending a new head and a full gate run on one clause.\n\nMergeable. The head is pushed, the working tree is clean, and it is cut from c80fbc61 with main now at bb710ea2, so the request and both artifacts carry ordinary staleness from the schema-only merge that landed after the request; neither artifact is retired and neither describes a superseded world, which is the fact that would block. Both are live — d31a9c5d at cmd/gs and e7f0cfd8 at docs — and this approval names both, so a receipt may retire each path's predecessor.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:d31a9c5d999c38b6e417720403f263da829fafad","head":"d4d4d7414da1b70740ebbb7afade6aa9d799bb44","stale":"true","staleness":"artifact, promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:439ff2b9a41801b43261065ccf7a6b6c26af1196, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:551b3d133dd7f5de2745921deb319988f08adb45, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:7b76d83d39c7216464116e1fb38cbf56630a2759, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:963745b8a557558de54fe9dd5d249ddf745691df and 2 more","verdict":"approved"}}